{"authored" [{"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/219d7aa5b976c97d47a96d0c94c3d61d139d5ddddc55b82d0df68f19ad6d5149.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [3,4]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}]-\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[9-1]-\\frac{1}{3}[27-1] \\\\  & =12-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}]+\\frac{1}{3}[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[16-9]+\\frac{1}{3}[64-27] \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}+\\frac{37}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{31}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{16}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{31}{6}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=27-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $3\\in [-3,5]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/6b3ca7be487585b296aaeb0b3fa7eb4ce8b9b4f9ad35d52baefa04f2fbbed621.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-3,3]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27x{|}_{-3}^{3}-{x}^{3}{|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =27[3-(-3)]-[(3)^{3}-(-3)^{3}] \\\\  & =162-54 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [3,5]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27x{|}_{3}^{5}+{x}^{3}{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-27[5-3]+[(5)^{3}-(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =-54+98 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=108+44=152.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "104"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "152"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "200"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "44"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "98"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "152"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/a4871287696f20009bd7d2b8793965c9f68e35faa4ad150477643f1d3ec77e38.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({x}^{2}-x-6) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-2,x=0$ or $x=3.$ Since $0\\in [-2,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-2}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/2a32d95d98a2c2cefe08b492c368a99df61a8cc3a1f66018a6dfe444e65146b8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(9-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3-x)(3+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 3$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-3,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-3,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,3]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{3}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-9}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{9}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-9}^{9}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "89c3db12f2607b4127e9bf48f745c3bf8fbdd1f0c085cfcff9294b383ce74076" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/57d01ea60e3cd9770f2ef978e46e2cef7b69a9fcdc2ac478d5b6c73d8f366c7b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3888.json" "file_sha256" "672a7a749017fbb69c0ce2f425e85fadf8bc58b978edb004b766c1a59329aebb" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=x^3-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3}-1 \\\\ (0) & ={x}^{3}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1 \\in [-2,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x={\\color{blue}{1}}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$ to $x=2.$\n\nSo, $a={\\color{blue}1}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{\\color{blue}1} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{\\color{blue}1}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3888.json" "file_sha256" "672a7a749017fbb69c0ce2f425e85fadf8bc58b978edb004b766c1a59329aebb" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/0d3ede3cfbec765228ea184c74e0cb35f1df2a9340bd1cb703a0990249fecf30.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3887.json" "file_sha256" "61cd36357b8a1b03cb152cd5acd36868b16c11f7caf656be6d354269a5dd8220" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0 \\in \\left[-1,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in  [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-1}^{0}  \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\nA_1 = -\\int_{-1}^{0}  \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nFor $x \\in\\left[0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]$, we have\n\n$$\nA_2 = \\int_{0}^{3/2}\\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 = - \\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{3/2} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{-1}^{0}+{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-[0-\\frac{1}{8}]+[\\frac{81}{128}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{81}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3887.json" "file_sha256" "61cd36357b8a1b03cb152cd5acd36868b16c11f7caf656be6d354269a5dd8220" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=x^3-9x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3889.json" "file_sha256" "b943aa10fa2aab6ef3ac30b7fe4a86228cf87a761f34948d461f527af937e133" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ x(x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0,$ $x=-3,$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/29a51c9892de59037e84bace2f8b5822222c3056a05af861cbd741390a437a61.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-9{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-3}^{0}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}]-9[0-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0]+9[\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =  \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3889.json" "file_sha256" "b943aa10fa2aab6ef3ac30b7fe4a86228cf87a761f34948d461f527af937e133" "basis" 1170 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=4{x}^{3}+4$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is our only real solution. Since $-1\\in [-2,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/b0f2899bf6a946f6d7bf8bc60c41827bd05aef500f0fc0f30a5916c26b1007bb.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-4x{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[(-1)^{4}-(-2)^{4}]-4[-1+2] \\\\  & =-(-15)-4 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+4x{|}_{-1}^{-1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{4}-(-1)^{4}]+4[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=11+8=19.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3384fc7b1aa85203a187daffb881854bbaa5c67aedfd469e6a181a068719b9c4" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "19"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/a8a85024d555378ad7ba31262a290386777fa94b00208d6a68fdacb5bb61d3c8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 1$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-1,1],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3384fc7b1aa85203a187daffb881854bbaa5c67aedfd469e6a181a068719b9c4" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/47e15993f3957fb3c1d7619fcf62544b2d5b358715538e17b9232dec5a0172c7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2$, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=2$ is $x=1.$\n\nSince $1\\in [0,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =([\\frac{32}{5}-2]-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{26}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}=6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3384fc7b1aa85203a187daffb881854bbaa5c67aedfd469e6a181a068719b9c4" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{21}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{22}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{26}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/state.json" "file_sha256" "36ef81b6b1e3878f3aa4eec5279b374eb773b00a4a9f560e446cde1693248b51" "basis" 1216 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-357604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/9542a7ddac8932b40e6f3a4217e7d97af6d1f3fc2598c595f9661ef5bc2b98ee.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{a}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x\\,-{\\int }_{a}^{3}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-357604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2} & =2 \\\\ 12 & =2({x}^{2}+2) \\\\ 12 & =2{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 8 & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ 4 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\pm 2 & =x \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=1$ and $x=3$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [1,3],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=3.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{2}^{3}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+2}-2)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-357604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-357604" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-357604" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/7d49ecfb67ace2bf8fa93b44c24796bbcc9953b3ee9976d30c9e50e28f01ff04.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-1$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{a}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{-1}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x+2)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-5$ and $x=-1$ is $x=-4.$\n\nSince $-4\\in [-5,-1],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-4.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-4$ to $x=-1.$\n\nSo, $a=-4$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{-4}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-140584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{15}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{17}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/a71e2e5e4cd2fddac779a7c2d21d072663f406bc6595ef9c7e15c885bbb83889.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=5$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{a}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{5}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{4}{x+3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{x+3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x+3} & =1 \\\\ 4 & =x+3 \\\\ 1 & =x \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-2,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=1$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-140579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/41e7b3f6588f7f4dd55096fb95bbe517a053c11f80c43aab171d80829864d2c3.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{a}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-2x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{2}\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}.$\n\nSo, $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{3/2}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-140574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-316137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/3aa68477e9e8f3bc688981cbd227e21af798364b8c0334ee9a22163b400b4064.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{a}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{a}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-316137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{4} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x-1)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm {9}^{1/4}\\,=\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-2$ and $x=0$ is $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSince $1-\\sqrt{3}\\in [-2,0],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1-\\sqrt{3}$ to $x=0.$\n\nSo, $a=1-\\sqrt{3}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{1-\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-316137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-316137" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-316137" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=4{x}^{3}+4$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is our only real solution. Since $-1\\in [-2,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/b0f2899bf6a946f6d7bf8bc60c41827bd05aef500f0fc0f30a5916c26b1007bb.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-4x{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[(-1)^{4}-(-2)^{4}]-4[-1+2] \\\\  & =-(-15)-4 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+4x{|}_{-1}^{-1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{4}-(-1)^{4}]+4[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=11+8=19.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11"] ["math" "19"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "19"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3{x}^{3}+24$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+24 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{3} & =-24 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-2\\in [-3,-1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/80315b4862adf8ebb1f91f5c4c7606a59dfa5738c40e34179a745e03a0a25693.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-3,-2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot ([4-16]-[\\frac{81}{4}-24]) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-12+\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{33}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{99}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,-1],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot ([\\frac{1}{4}-8]-[4-16]) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{4}+12) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{99}{4}+\\frac{51}{4}=\\frac{75}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-117442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "60"] ["math" "70"] ["math" "75"] ["math" "\\frac{75}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{75}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{75}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/47e15993f3957fb3c1d7619fcf62544b2d5b358715538e17b9232dec5a0172c7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2$, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=2$ is $x=1.$\n\nSince $1\\in [0,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =([\\frac{32}{5}-2]-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{26}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}=6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{22}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{26}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/219d7aa5b976c97d47a96d0c94c3d61d139d5ddddc55b82d0df68f19ad6d5149.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [3,4]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}]-\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[9-1]-\\frac{1}{3}[27-1] \\\\  & =12-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}]+\\frac{1}{3}[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[16-9]+\\frac{1}{3}[64-27] \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}+\\frac{37}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{31}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{31}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{31}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-361717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/fc09b3b302a0920562a3ca03f52a2f6f98dc327b89f231e07b8a6ab631a48b2b.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-361717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [1,3]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}+{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[{3}^{4}-{1}^{4}]+[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[81-1]+[27-1] \\\\  & =-20+26 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}-{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{4}^{4}-{3}^{4}]-[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[256-81]-[64-27] \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{4}-37 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{4}+\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-361717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-361717" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-361717" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{51}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/a4871287696f20009bd7d2b8793965c9f68e35faa4ad150477643f1d3ec77e38.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({x}^{2}-x-6) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-2,x=0$ or $x=3.$ Since $0\\in [-2,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=27-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $3\\in [-3,5]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/6b3ca7be487585b296aaeb0b3fa7eb4ce8b9b4f9ad35d52baefa04f2fbbed621.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-3,3]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27x{|}_{-3}^{3}-{x}^{3}{|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =27[3-(-3)]-[(3)^{3}-(-3)^{3}] \\\\  & =162-54 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [3,5]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27x{|}_{3}^{5}+{x}^{3}{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-27[5-3]+[(5)^{3}-(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =-54+98 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=108+44=152.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "104"] ["math" "152"] ["math" "200"] ["math" "44"] ["math" "98"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "152"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/2a32d95d98a2c2cefe08b492c368a99df61a8cc3a1f66018a6dfe444e65146b8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(9-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3-x)(3+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 3$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-3,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-3,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,3]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{3}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-9}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{9}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-9}^{9}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/8b7b81c97a842b82c2e4cbbd041883c1bd7f4f4ab58dc6592c12ec1cc0262620.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x(x+1)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-1,x=0$ or $x=3.$ Since $0\\in [-1,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-140570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-140570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/a8a85024d555378ad7ba31262a290386777fa94b00208d6a68fdacb5bb61d3c8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 1$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-1,1],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "382ae6859e2ac96b107064b2afdba14f67b958fbe84c4fdcec573912c5f7e057"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "q-54482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/state.json" "file_sha256" "4ec56beb8773cd89f9310ec7f0f49cf84c638ea686753a344cee287927b5b0fd"} {"question" "e-3888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/57d01ea60e3cd9770f2ef978e46e2cef7b69a9fcdc2ac478d5b6c73d8f366c7b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/example-3888.json" "file_sha256" "a7488342d54f5e41d424ae4d2f3ebf9574e80c4a8c51c39ac3d5e66ca393212d"} {"question" "e-3888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=x^3-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3}-1 \\\\ (0) & ={x}^{3}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1 \\in [-2,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x={\\color{blue}{1}}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$ to $x=2.$\n\nSo, $a={\\color{blue}1}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{\\color{blue}1} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{\\color{blue}1}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/example-3888.json" "file_sha256" "a7488342d54f5e41d424ae4d2f3ebf9574e80c4a8c51c39ac3d5e66ca393212d"} {"question" "e-3887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/0d3ede3cfbec765228ea184c74e0cb35f1df2a9340bd1cb703a0990249fecf30.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/example-3887.json" "file_sha256" "b911f868a9b30bdb702589d028c8927c0c533047ac42e5f96599dfb4c9fde4e4"} {"question" "e-3887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0 \\in \\left[-1,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in  [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-1}^{0}  \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\nA_1 = -\\int_{-1}^{0}  \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nFor $x \\in\\left[0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]$, we have\n\n$$\nA_2 = \\int_{0}^{3/2}\\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 = - \\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{3/2} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{-1}^{0}+{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-[0-\\frac{1}{8}]+[\\frac{81}{128}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{81}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/example-3887.json" "file_sha256" "b911f868a9b30bdb702589d028c8927c0c533047ac42e5f96599dfb4c9fde4e4"} {"question" "e-3889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=x^3-9x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/example-3889.json" "file_sha256" "9411801fa884fbe80efdc7ad8981067dee84e8fb56dc6c180337746833f5a37a"} {"question" "e-3889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ x(x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0,$ $x=-3,$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/29a51c9892de59037e84bace2f8b5822222c3056a05af861cbd741390a437a61.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-9{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-3}^{0}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}]-9[0-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0]+9[\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =  \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/example-3889.json" "file_sha256" "9411801fa884fbe80efdc7ad8981067dee84e8fb56dc6c180337746833f5a37a"}] "usage" {"q-140577" [] "e-3889" [] "e-3887" [] "q-117447" [] "q-140580" [] "q-54482" [] "e-3888" [] "q-140607" [] "q-54476" [] "q-54494" []} "basis" 2846 "retractions" [[17592186293263 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is our only real solution. Since $-1\\in [-2,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/b0f2899bf6a946f6d7bf8bc60c41827bd05aef500f0fc0f30a5916c26b1007bb.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-4x{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[(-1)^{4}-(-2)^{4}]-4[-1+2] \\\\  & =-(-15)-4 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+4x{|}_{-1}^{-1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{4}-(-1)^{4}]+4[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=11+8=19.\n$$"] [17592186293270 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/47e15993f3957fb3c1d7619fcf62544b2d5b358715538e17b9232dec5a0172c7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2$, as shown above."] [17592186291324 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/219d7aa5b976c97d47a96d0c94c3d61d139d5ddddc55b82d0df68f19ad6d5149.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [3,4]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}]-\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[9-1]-\\frac{1}{3}[27-1] \\\\  & =12-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}]+\\frac{1}{3}[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[16-9]+\\frac{1}{3}[64-27] \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}+\\frac{37}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{31}{6}.\n$$"] [17592186291289 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/a4871287696f20009bd7d2b8793965c9f68e35faa4ad150477643f1d3ec77e38.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?"] [17592186291282 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $3\\in [-3,5]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/6b3ca7be487585b296aaeb0b3fa7eb4ce8b9b4f9ad35d52baefa04f2fbbed621.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-3,3]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27x{|}_{-3}^{3}-{x}^{3}{|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =27[3-(-3)]-[(3)^{3}-(-3)^{3}] \\\\  & =162-54 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [3,5]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27x{|}_{3}^{5}+{x}^{3}{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-27[5-3]+[(5)^{3}-(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =-54+98 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=108+44=152.\n$$"] [17592186291296 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/2a32d95d98a2c2cefe08b492c368a99df61a8cc3a1f66018a6dfe444e65146b8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?"] [17592186111249 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/a8a85024d555378ad7ba31262a290386777fa94b00208d6a68fdacb5bb61d3c8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?"] [17592186110406 :question/problem "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/57d01ea60e3cd9770f2ef978e46e2cef7b69a9fcdc2ac478d5b6c73d8f366c7b.png)"] [17592186110405 :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/0d3ede3cfbec765228ea184c74e0cb35f1df2a9340bd1cb703a0990249fecf30.png)"] [17592186110407 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ x(x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0,$ $x=-3,$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/29a51c9892de59037e84bace2f8b5822222c3056a05af861cbd741390a437a61.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-9{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-3}^{0}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}]-9[0-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0]+9[\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =  \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}.\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186109590 17592186109591 17592186109592 17592186109593 17592186109594 17592186291284 17592186291285 17592186291286 17592186291287 17592186291288 17592186291291 17592186291292 17592186291293 17592186291294 17592186291295 17592186291298 17592186291299 17592186291300 17592186291301 17592186291302 17592186291326 17592186291327 17592186291328 17592186291329 17592186291330 17592186293265 17592186293266 17592186293267 17592186293268 17592186293269 17592186293272 17592186293273 17592186293274 17592186293275 17592186293276] "immutable_fields" [[17592186109589 :answer-field/correct] [17592186109589 :answer-field/id] [17592186109589 :answer-field/key] [17592186109589 :answer-field/type] [17592186291283 :answer-field/correct] [17592186291283 :answer-field/id] [17592186291283 :answer-field/key] [17592186291283 :answer-field/type] [17592186291290 :answer-field/correct] [17592186291290 :answer-field/id] [17592186291290 :answer-field/key] [17592186291290 :answer-field/type] [17592186291297 :answer-field/correct] [17592186291297 :answer-field/id] [17592186291297 :answer-field/key] [17592186291297 :answer-field/type] [17592186291325 :answer-field/correct] [17592186291325 :answer-field/id] [17592186291325 :answer-f